        "body": "`requireContentType()` documents a 422 response for malformed Content-Type values, but only checks whether the type has a nonempty substring on either side of its first slash. Wildcard acceptance therefore lets malformed media types through.\n\nReproduced on current main, Node 24:\n\n```ts\nimport { H3, requireContentType } from 'h3'\n\nconst app = new H3()\napp.post('/', event => ({\n  mediaType: requireContentType(event, ['application/*']),\n}))\n\nconst response = await app.fetch(new Request('http://localhost/', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json/extra' },\n}))\nconsole.log(response.status) // 200; expected 422\n```\n\n`application/json junk` and `application/(json)` are also accepted by that wildcard. With an exact accepted type, malformed values reach the unsupported-type branch instead of the documented malformed-type branch.\n\nThe type and subtype must each be an HTTP token ([RFC 9110, sections 8.3.1 and 5.6.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-media-type)). I plan to add complete type/subtype token validation before matching accepted types, with regression tests in both web and Node modes plus valid punctuation/parameter/wildcard controls. No parameter-parsing or API changes are intended. This work is AI-assisted.\n",
